<v Rob>Yes, of course you have--
<v Rob>是的，当然你有——

00:39:02.720 --> 00:39:04.670
<v Raymond Buckley>I still have to ask, Rob.
<v Raymond Buckley>我还是得问问，罗布。

00:39:04.670 --> 00:39:17.430
<v Sarah Koenig>Buckley was back in the race. The governor endorsed him again. I met with Buckley at the headquarters of the Manchester Democrats, eight days before the election. Buckley was working his way down a list of 196 names in tiny print.
<v Sarah Koenig>巴克利回到了竞选中。州长再次支持了他。在选举前八天，我在曼彻斯特民主党总部会见了巴克利。巴克利正在一份印着小字的196人名单上一个接一个地打。

00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:24.210
<v Raymond Buckley>Let me make another call. I'm in the zone here. Hi, Jerry. It's Ray Buckley, how are you?
<v Raymond Buckley>让我再打一个电话。我进入状态了。嗨，杰瑞。我是雷·巴克利，你好吗？

00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:26.140
<v Jerry>I'm fine, Ray, how are you?
<v Jerry>我很好，雷，你呢？

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:28.670
<v Raymond Buckley>I'm terrific now. [LAUGHING]
<v Raymond Buckley>我现在好极了。[大笑]

00:39:28.670 --> 00:39:49.190
<v Sarah Koenig>It was going well. But while I was sitting there in his office, Buckley's cellphone started going nuts, buzzing every minute or two. He'd glance at it, and then keep talking. Until a call came from the current chair of the party, Kathy Sullivan. He spoke to her for a second, and then asked me to leave the room. And while I was standing outside his closed door, a local reporter called me and tipped me off about what was happening.
<v Sarah Koenig>进行得很顺利。但当我坐在他的办公室里时，巴克利的手机开始疯狂响动，每分钟左右嗡嗡作响。他会瞥一眼，然后继续说话。直到党主席凯西·沙利文打来电话。他和她说了几秒钟，然后让我离开房间。当我站在他紧闭的门外时，一名当地记者给我打电话，向我透露了正在发生的事情。

00:39:49.190 --> 00:40:10.080
<v Sarah Koenig>Paul Hodes, the newly elected congressman, one of the state's top Democrats, had just issued a press release saying he was withdrawing his support for Buckley's candidacy because he'd seen a video of Buckley posted on YouTube. Hodes also said he thought the proper authorities should look into the matter. Suddenly, Buckley's candidacy was in jeopardy all over again.
<v Sarah Koenig>新当选的国会议员保罗·霍兹（Paul Hodes），该州最高级别的民主党人之一，刚刚发表了一份新闻稿，称他撤回对巴克利竞选的支持，因为他看到了Youtube上发布的巴克利的视频。霍兹还表示，他认为有关当局应该调查此事。突然间，巴克利的竞选再次岌岌可危。

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:31.120
<v Sarah Koenig>The YouTube thing had been posted the day before. Vaillancourt didn't post it himself, but he gave another Manchester Republican some old home movies for it. It showed Buckley in his 20s and 30s behaving not badly exactly, but embarrassingly. Doing things maybe a gay frat boy would do, and saying things you might say if you were on vacation with your friends, or playing a board game late at night. Which he was.
<v Sarah Koenig>YouTube的事情是前一天发布的。瓦兰考特本人没有发布它，但他给了另一个曼彻斯特共和党人一些旧的家庭录像用于发布。录像显示巴克利在20多岁和30多岁时表现得并不算坏，但很尴尬。做着也许同性恋兄弟会成员会做的事，说着如果你和朋友度假或深夜玩棋盘游戏时可能会说的话。他确实在玩。

00:40:31.120 --> 00:40:32.890
<v Raymond Buckley>Would you like me to [BLEEP] you hard?
<v Raymond Buckley>你想让我[哔——]你硬吗？

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:34.730
<v Steve Vaillancourt>About this trip to the Hague.
<v Steve Vaillancourt>关于这次去海牙的旅行。

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:36.200
<v Raymond Buckley>Hitler should have bombed it.
<v Raymond Buckley>希特勒早该炸了它。

00:40:36.200 --> 00:40:45.540
<v Sarah Koenig>This is from Vaillancourt's old home movies. And what's striking is how intimate they are. Even though the content is awful. He and Buckley were such close friends when he shot these movies.
<v Sarah Koenig>这是来自瓦兰考特的旧家庭录像。引人注目的是它们是多么私密。即使内容很糟糕。他拍摄这些电影时，他和巴克利是如此亲密的朋友。

00:40:45.540 --> 00:40:50.470
<v Steve Vaillancourt>Put little clips on them and pull you down the [BLEEP] street right by your little nipples.
<v Steve Vaillancourt>把小夹子夹在上面，把你拉到[哔——]街上，就在你那点小乳头上。

00:40:50.470 --> 00:41:08.970
<v Sarah Koenig>In another part of the video, a narrator points out that Buckley has a MySpace page that's just a few clicks away from gay teenagers. When I came back into the room, Buckley was trying to call his staff members back to work. He needed to send out an urgent email to party members to try to neutralize Hodes' statement. It was a Friday night, and there was a blizzard.
<v Sarah Koenig>在视频的另一部分，旁白指出巴克利有一个MySpace页面，距离同性恋青少年只有几次点击的距离。当我回到房间时，巴克利正试图让他的员工回到工作岗位。他需要给党内成员发送紧急电子邮件，试图抵消霍兹的声明。那是周五晚上，而且有暴风雪。

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:23.560
<v Raymond Buckley>Yeah. Hey, can you help me try to track down Michael so he can get back here? I can't hear you, Donna. His car probably isn't going to drive very well in this snow, but I need to send out an email. I'm at the city Dems office.
<v Raymond Buckley>对。嘿，你能帮我查查迈克尔在哪吗，这样他能回来？我听不见你说话，唐娜。他的车在这雪里可能开得不太好，但我需要发送一封电子邮件。我在市民主党办公室。

00:41:23.560 --> 00:41:41.830
<v Sarah Koenig>I had asked Buckley about the YouTube clip the day before, when it was posted, and he laughed it off, saying the worst thing about it was that he realized how much he'd aged. The newspapers didn't seem interested either, and they didn't bother writing about it. Now Hodes had just made it a story, by saying he's withdrawing his support for Buckley because of it. And Buckley had to fight back.
<v Sarah Koenig>前一天视频发布时，我问过巴克利关于那个YouTube剪辑的事，他大笑着否定了，说最糟糕的是他意识到自己变老了多少。报纸似乎也没兴趣，他们也没费心写报道。现在霍兹刚刚通过说因为这件事撤回对巴克利的支持，把它变成了一个新闻。巴克利不得不反击。

00:41:41.830 --> 00:41:44.920
<v Raymond Buckley>I dropped out once, I'm not doing it again.
<v Raymond Buckley>我退出过一次，我不会再退出了。

00:41:44.920 --> 00:42:02.810
<v Sarah Koenig>Two staffers came in and went straight to their desks, as if they already knew what to do. Buckley sat down at a laptop. At this moment, he's fighting for his career. For his life's work, really. And what that entails, couldn't be more annoyingly small. He's trying to log on to YouTube and extract a six-minute video from cyberspace.
<v Sarah Koenig>两个员工进来，直接走向他们的办公桌，好像他们已经知道该怎么做。巴克利坐在笔记本电脑前。在这一刻，他正在为自己的职业生涯而战。真的，为了他一生的事业。而这所涉及的事情，再也没有比这更烦人的小事了。他正试图登录YouTube并从网络空间提取一段六分钟的视频。

00:42:02.810 --> 00:42:09.300
<v Raymond Buckley>Don't touch. Because this is-- how do you even type on this thing?
<v Raymond Buckley>别碰。因为这……你怎么在这个东西上打字？

00:42:09.300 --> 00:42:11.040
<v Michael>2006.
<v Michael>2006。

00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:13.210
<v Raymond Buckley>No, it's my birthday. I was not born in 2006, Michael.
<v Raymond Buckley>不，那是我的生日。我不是2006年出生的，迈克尔。

00:42:13.210 --> 00:42:14.920
<v Michael>I was kidding, sir.
<v Michael>我是在开玩笑，先生。

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:15.350
<v Raymond Buckley>Oh.
<v Raymond Buckley>噢。

00:42:15.350 --> 00:42:35.950
<v Sarah Koenig>No one's saying much. They all seem utterly focused and purposeful, like a special ops team. Reporters are calling and Buckley is not picking up. And friends, a couple of whom are lawyers, are calling to give advice. It occurs to me that Buckley's probably had to do this dozens of times for other candidates. Politics is all about tearing down reputations and building them back up. So of course he knows what to do for his own crisis.
<v Sarah Koenig>没人说话。他们看起来都非常专注和有目的，就像一支特种作战小组。记者们在打电话，巴克利没接。朋友们，其中几个是律师，打电话来提供建议。我想到巴克利可能已经为其他候选人做过几十次这种事了。政治就是关于摧毁名声并重新建立它们。所以他当然知道如何应对自己的危机。

00:42:35.950 --> 00:42:45.370
<v Sarah Koenig>They find out they can't shut down the video, but they can complain about it online. So their next strategy is to get their friends to become YouTube members, so they can flag the video as offensive.
<v Sarah Koenig>他们发现无法关闭视频，但他们可以在网上投诉。所以他们的下一个策略是让朋友成为YouTube成员，这样他们就可以把视频标记为冒犯性的。

00:42:45.370 --> 00:42:53.240
<v Raymond Buckley>You know, Chris is at home. Obviously, Donna's at home. Call Judy. Just have them start calling people.
<v Raymond Buckley>你知道，克里斯在家。显然，唐娜在家。打电话给朱迪。让他们开始联系人。

00:42:53.240 --> 00:43:18.420
<v Sarah Koenig>After about 40 minutes, they give up on Youtube, and concentrate instead on the email they're writing to committee members. Michael is on the phone with an adviser, and they decide on what the message should be. That at the time these movies were filmed, Buckley was young and immature. Michael suggests they fib about his age, and say the video was shot when Buckley was in his 20s. Buckley doesn't take his advice. He sidesteps how old he was and instead concentrates on the bigger question, who should they blame for the attack?
<v Sarah Koenig>大约40分钟后，他们放弃了YouTube，转而集中精力写给委员会成员的邮件。迈克尔正在和一位顾问通话，他们决定了信息应该是什么内容。在那次电影拍摄时，巴克利还年轻且不成熟。迈克尔建议他们在年龄上撒谎，说视频是巴克利20多岁时拍摄的。巴克利没接受他的建议。他回避了年龄问题，转而集中讨论更大的问题，谁应该为这次攻击负责？

00:43:18.420 --> 00:43:25.450
<v Sarah Koenig>Party chairwoman Kathy Sullivan is on the phone, and she proposes blaming the Republican party as a whole. But Buckley isn't so sure.
<v Sarah Koenig>党主席凯西·沙利文在电话上，她提议责备整个共和党。但巴克利不太确定。

00:43:25.450 --> 00:43:41.910
<v Raymond Buckley>Well, think about it. Because if it's clearly pinpointed, it's just the two of them operating on their own, as these two rogue, disgraced individuals. I guess I don't want to make this entirely about me against the Republican party.
<v Raymond Buckley>嗯，考虑一下。因为如果明确指出，这只是他们两个人自己在操作，作为这两个流氓、丢脸的个人。我想我不想让这完全变成我和共和党之间的对抗。

00:43:41.910 --> 00:43:44.030
<v Michael>You sure about that?
<v Michael>你确定吗？

00:43:44.030 --> 00:43:56.500
<v Sarah Koenig>That night the YouTube video had only been seen 365 times. Despite everything Buckley's people did, the thing stayed in the news for days. And by the end of the week, it had been seen 7,000 more times.
<v Sarah Koenig>那天晚上YouTube视频只被观看了365次。尽管巴克利的人做了所有尝试，这件事还是在新闻中停留了几天。到周末结束时，它已经被观看了7000多次。

00:43:56.500 --> 00:44:01.070
<v Woman>If all of you members of the state committee could please take your seats.
<v Woman>如果各位州委员会成员能请就座。

00:44:01.070 --> 00:44:04.670
<v Sarah Koenig>Finally, the state party had its meeting, where they would elect the new chairman.
<v Sarah Koenig>最终，州党召开了会议，他们将在会上选举新主席。

00:44:04.670 --> 00:44:07.650
<v Woman>Oh, Martha's writing a check. Then we won't have her take her seat.
<v Woman>噢，玛莎正在写支票。那我们就不让她就座了。

00:44:07.650 --> 00:44:23.280
<v Sarah Koenig>This was the moment Buckley had been fighting for three months. The moment that would decide whether he'd get his dream job. Whether he'd really survived. There was a little skirmish when congressman Paul Hodes, the guy who had started the Youtube flap, gave his speech. But mostly it was pretty civil. Eventually Kathy Sullivan took the mic.
<v Sarah Koenig>这就是巴克利三个月来一直战斗的时刻。决定他是否能得到梦想工作的时刻。他是否真的活下来了。当YouTube风波发起者保罗·霍兹国会议员发表演讲时，有一点小冲突。但大多很文明。最终凯西·沙利文拿起了麦克风。

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:33.490
<v Kathy Sullivan>I have the results. Our new chairman, with a vote of 109, is Raymond Buckley.
<v Kathy Sullivan>我有结果了。我们的新主席，以109票当选，是雷蒙德·巴克利。

00:44:33.490 --> 00:44:37.230
<v Kathy Sullivan>[APPLAUSE]
<v Kathy Sullivan>[掌声]

00:44:37.230 --> 00:44:45.340
<v Sarah Koenig>After all the cheering, reporters mobbed Buckley, who talked about how, as the new chairman, he wanted to improve civility in political life in New Hampshire.
<v Sarah Koenig>欢呼之后，记者们围住了巴克利，他谈到作为新主席，他想改善新罕布什尔州政治生活中的文明。

00:44:45.340 --> 00:44:58.440
<v Raymond Buckley>After the three months that I've just lived through, it's really my mission now. There's no way that you could've survived the few months that I did without coming out a changed person.
<v Raymond Buckley>在我刚刚经历过三个月之后，这真的是我现在使命。没有改变，你就不可能度过那几个月。

00:44:58.440 --> 00:45:12.180
<v Sarah Koenig>Throughout this whole thing, people kept talking about the politics of personal destruction, and how shocked they were that these tactics had come to New Hampshire. But in a presidential primary year especially, it's hard to be optimistic that people will suddenly be nicer to each other.
<v Sarah Koenig>在整个过程中，人们一直在谈论人身破坏政治，以及他们对这些策略来到新罕布什尔州感到多么震惊。但在总统初选年尤其如此，很难乐观地认为人们会突然对彼此更好。

00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:31.900
<v Sarah Koenig>The real change to come out of all this public, political drama, will probably end up being private and personal. Because even though his career might have survived, Buckley is changed. He told me he no longer trusts people the way he used to. He no longer wants to make new friends. He polices himself all the time, making sure he doesn't say anything that someone else might find offensive.
<v Sarah Koenig>从所有这些公共政治戏剧中产生的真正改变，最终很可能是私人和个人的。因为即使他的事业可能幸存下来了，巴克利也变了。他告诉我他不再像过去那样信任别人。他不再想交新朋友。他一直监督自己，确保自己不说任何别人可能觉得冒犯的话。

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:54.520
<v Raymond Buckley>The other day at lunch, a staff person used extreme language to reference a person. And I just quickly picked up what was left of my lunch and I said, "I have to leave the room, because you said that word. I have to leave."
<v Raymond Buckley>前几天午餐时，一名员工使用极端语言来指代一个人。我很快收拾好午餐剩下的东西说，“我得离开房间，因为你说了那个词。我得走。”

00:45:54.520 --> 00:45:56.720
<v Sarah Koenig>Whereas before you would have laughed or just--
<v Sarah Koenig>而以前你会笑，或者只是……

00:45:56.720 --> 00:46:10.590
<v Raymond Buckley>I wouldn't pay attention, I wouldn't be listening. Now I listen to everything that's going on around me, to make sure that nobody can misinterpret anything that anyone is saying near me that was inappropriate.
<v Raymond Buckley>我不会注意，我不会听。现在我倾听周围发生的一切，以确保没有人可以曲解任何在他附近说的任何不恰当的话。

00:46:10.590 --> 00:46:21.440
<v Sarah Koenig>Because the attorney general's report was so emphatic about clearing him, and because the report was such big news, his reputation's about as good as he could hope for. Some people even said he came out looking stronger for all this.
<v Sarah Koenig>因为总检察长的报告对洗清他的嫌疑非常强调，而且报告是如此大的新闻，他的名声比他希望的要好。有些人甚至说他因为这一切看起来更坚强了。

00:46:21.440 --> 00:46:28.450
<v Sarah Koenig>But Buckley thinks he's permanently changed. That he's not sure he'll ever really feel in control of his life again.
<v Sarah Koenig>但巴克利认为他永久改变了。他不确定是否能真正再次掌控自己的生活。

00:46:28.450 --> 00:46:31.590
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Koenig is a producer of our show.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉·科尼格是我们节目的制片人。

00:46:31.590 --> 00:47:43.140
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, what if you have a terrible reputation, and then you figure out that it is absolutely correct. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，如果你有名声很臭，然后你发现它是完全正确的怎么办。一会儿就来，来自芝加哥公共广播和公共广播国际，我们的节目继续。

00:47:43.140 --> 00:47:57.720
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life , I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring your a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show "My Reputation." We've arrived at act two of our show, act two. The Hole Truth.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，我们选择一个主题，带来各种不同类型的关于那个主题的故事。今天的节目“我的名声”。我们到达了我们节目的第二幕，第二幕。“真相大白”。

00:47:57.720 --> 00:48:16.290
<v Ira Glass>I think most of us would rather not think about our reputations tpp much, you know? What's the point? What's it going to do for you? But in this next story one man, one brave man, faces his reputation. He sets out to learn, once and for all, what people really think of him. This brave man's name is Gabe Delahaye.
<v Ira Glass>我想我们大多数人更愿意不太多想我们的名声，你知道吗？有什么意义？它会为你做什么？但在下一个故事中，一个人，一个勇敢的人，面对他的名声。他出发去了解，一劳永逸地，人们对他真正的想法。这个勇敢的人名叫盖布·德拉海（Gabe Delahaye）。

00:48:16.290 --> 00:48:21.360
<v Ira Glass>A warning to listeners that a word shows up in this story. A word that we beep.
<v Ira Glass>提醒听众，这个故事中会出现一个词。我们消音的一个词。

00:48:21.360 --> 00:48:33.710
<v Gabriel Delahaye>I'm well aware that I have a certain reputation. My roommate, Andrew, who went to high school with me and knows me better than almost anyone, who is one of the most thoughtful and considerate people I know, puts it best.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>我很清楚我有一个特定的名声。我的室友安德鲁（Andrew），跟我上高中，比任何人更了解我，是我认识的最深思熟虑和体贴的人之一，他总结得最好。

00:48:33.710 --> 00:48:54.150
<v Andrew>This is actually really hard to say to somebody's face, it turns out. I mean everyone-- I don't know. Sorry, I am actually trying to figure out the best way to do this. I don't know, everyone just thinks you're an ass[BLEEP].
<v Andrew>这实际上很难当面说，事实证明。我是说每个人……我不知道。对不起，我实际上正在尝试找出最好的方式来做这个。我不知道，每个人都认为你是混蛋[哔——]。

00:48:54.150 --> 00:49:20.870
<v Gabriel Delahaye>I mean, it's true that I make fun of people all the time. But with my friends, at least, it comes from a good place. Like that time that Danielle asked me at the bar if I'd seen that episode of 90210 and I shouted "No," before she could even tell me which one. And then I laughed in her face. And, OK, I'd just met her that night. So Lindsey turned to me and said that she wanted me to teach her how to be such an ass[BLEEP]. And I told her it couldn't be taught. But we were all just joking around, right?
<v Gabriel Delahaye>我是说，确实我总是取笑别人。但至少在我的朋友们那里，它来自一个好地方。就像那次丹妮尔（Danielle）在酒吧问我是否看过那集《飞越比弗利》时，我还没等她告诉我哪一集就大喊“没有”。然后我对着她的脸大笑。噢，好吧，我那天晚上才刚见到她。所以琳赛（Lindsey）转过身对我说，她想让我教她如何成为一个混蛋[哔——]。我告诉她这没法教。但我们都在开玩笑，对吧？

00:49:20.870 --> 00:49:39.160
<v Gabriel Delahaye>That's how I saw it. And I assumed my friends saw it that way, too. I mean, Andrew calls me an ass[BLEEP] all the time. He warns people about me, right in front of me. But he wouldn't still be my friend if it wasn't kind of a joke. It's just this role that I've been assigned in our group of friends, a role I'm more than happy to play.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>那就是我看它的方式。我以为我的朋友们也那样看。我是说，安德鲁一直叫我混蛋[哔——]。他就在我面前警告别人关于我，对吧？但他不会仍然是我的朋友如果它不是一种玩笑。这就是我在我们的朋友群中被分配到的角色，一个我非常乐意扮演的角色。

00:49:39.160 --> 00:49:53.980
<v Gabriel Delahaye>So I sat Andrew down, to make sure he felt the same way. That I'm a quote, unquote, ass[BLEEP]. But I'm not really an ass[BLEEP]. And, right away, he brings up the emails that led to this very conversation. I had written to ask him if he could be home at 8:00, to talk on tape.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>所以我让安德鲁坐下来，确定他有同样的感受。我是个所谓的混蛋[哔——]。但我不是真正的混蛋[哔——]。而且，立刻，他提起了导致这次对话的邮件。我写信问他是否可以在8点回家，在磁带上谈谈。

00:49:53.980 --> 00:50:24.120
<v Andrew>And at this point, I say, "All right, that sounds pretty good. Make sure to tell craft services I am ovo-lacto," which is maybe not the funniest joke but, you know. And you reply, I think out of nowhere, "Sure. How do you take your coffee? Like your women, right? So that's rarely." And then you write, "Zing." I thought the whole thing was a little unnecessary.
<v Andrew>而在这一点上，我说，“好吧，那听起来不错。一定要告诉制作服务我是蛋奶素，”这也许不是最有趣的笑话，你知道。而你回复，我想完全出乎意料，“当然。你咖啡怎么喝？像你的女人一样吗？很少。”然后你写，“叮。”我觉得整件事有点没必要。

00:50:24.120 --> 00:50:31.840
<v Gabriel Delahaye>But you don't, uh, drink a lot of coffee. Right?
<v Gabriel Delahaye>但你并不，呃，喝很多咖啡。对吧？

00:50:31.840 --> 00:50:40.620
<v Andrew>Yeah, that's true.
<v Andrew>是的，那是真的。

00:50:40.620 --> 00:50:45.080
<v Gabriel Delahaye>Have you ever seen me be nice to people?
<v Gabriel Delahaye>你见过我对人友善吗？

00:50:45.080 --> 00:51:02.390
<v Andrew>Yeah. I can't think of any examples, but I'm sure you have. I'm sure you have. Like if you weren't paying attention or something. You've probably gotten up on the subway for somebody or something, right? Maybe not.
<v Andrew>是的。我想不出任何例子，但我确定你有。我确定你有。就像如果你没注意或什么。你可能在地铁上给别人让座或什么，对吧？也许没有。

00:51:02.390 --> 00:51:04.760
<v Gabriel Delahaye>Why are you still friends with me?
<v Gabriel Delahaye>为什么你还是我的朋友？

00:51:04.760 --> 00:51:07.970
<v Andrew>I don't know. Inertia?
<v Andrew>我不知道。惯性？

00:51:07.970 --> 00:51:21.800
<v Gabriel Delahaye>Inertia. Did you hear him say that he was only friends with me out of inertia? And I'm the ass[BLEEP]?
<v Gabriel Delahaye>惯性。你听到他说他和我做朋友只是出于惯性吗？我是混蛋[哔——]？

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:39.290
<v Gabriel Delahaye>So we're sitting there talking about this stuff, and our friend Travis, who lives in the neighborhood, showed up. And once the two of them could compare notes, something changed. For one, they didn't just think I was an ass[BLEEP] because of a well meaning joke taken too far. It was more than that. Like the time Travis wouldn't take off his stupid bicycle helmet.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>所以我们坐在那里谈论这些东西，我们的朋友特拉维斯（Travis），住在附近，出现了。一旦他们俩能对比笔记，事情就变了。一方面，他们不只是认为我是混蛋[哔——]，因为一个善意的玩笑走得太远了。它不仅仅是那样。就像那次特拉维斯不肯脱下他愚蠢的自行车头盔。

00:51:39.290 --> 00:52:01.960
<v Travis>I think I was carrying something else. Like, it was just easier to just put the helmet on my head, even though I wasn't on my bike. But for some reason, that prompted you to keeps hitting me on my head. Which, you know, it was kind of funny the first time but it actually kind of hurt. And I was, like, stop doing that. So I was telling someone else about it. Like, can you believe what a jerk this guy is? Like, why is he your friend? It's like, well.
<v Travis>我想我正带着别的东西。就像，把头盔戴在头上更容易，即使我没骑车。但由于某种原因，那促使你不停地敲我的头。这，你知道，第一次还有点好笑，但它实际上有点痛。我当时想，停止那样做。所以我告诉了别人。就像，你能相信这人是多大的混蛋吗？为什么他是你的朋友？就像，嗯。

00:52:01.960 --> 00:52:05.750
<v Gabriel Delahaye>I was telling you to take the helmet off, though.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>不过我在让你把头盔拿下来。

00:52:05.750 --> 00:52:11.840
<v Travis>Yeah, but that's not a good way to tell someone to take the helmet off.
<v Travis>是的，但那不是让人把头盔拿下来的好方法。

00:52:11.840 --> 00:52:24.160
<v Gabriel Delahaye>He was right, that was kind of bad. It was like in elementary school when someone would grab your hand and start hitting you in the face with your own hand, and ask you why you were hitting yourself. Who does that? Apparently, I do.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>他是对的，那有点糟糕。这就像在小学，有人会抓住你的手开始用你自己的手打你的脸，问你为什么打你自己。谁会那样做？显然，我做了。

00:52:24.160 --> 00:52:45.660
<v Gabriel Delahaye>And the more they talked, the more obvious it became that their feelings about my behavior went much deeper than just thinking I can be an ass[BLEEP]. They admitted that lots of times they'll email each other all day long without including me, because they figure I'll just make fun of them. And at that moment, in the middle of a question, I had one of those realizations in which you see something you thought you understood in a totally new light.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>他们谈论得越多，他们对我的行为的感觉远远超过仅仅认为我可以是一个混蛋[哔——]变得越明显。他们承认很多时候他们会整天给彼此发邮件而不包括我，因为他们觉得我只会取笑他们。而在那一刻，在问题中间，我有了一次那种领悟，你以全新的视角看待你以为你理解的东西。

00:52:45.660 --> 00:53:19.120
<v Gabriel Delahaye>I find that there have been countless situations where I'll call you, and you're out with everybody. And no one has called me. Now that could be because I'm an ass[BLEEP] and you guys don't want to hang out with me, I'm now realizing. Only now. But that's a very-- that's always been very hurtful to me.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>我发现有无数次情况我会打电话给你，你和大家在一起。而没有人打给我。现在这可能是因为我是混蛋[哔——]，你们不想和我混在一起，我现在意识到了。只有现在。但这一个……那对我一直都很伤人。

00:53:19.120 --> 00:53:48.770
<v Ira Glass>It's hard to describe what this was like. The room actually seemed to shrink. Of course it wasn't that every single one of my friends forgot to invite me, repeatedly. Of course. And maybe it was the uncomfortable laughter, but they hardly noticed that my face was red, and I was drenched in sweat. I could imagine that an acquaintance, or somebody who had just met me, might feel this way about me. But these were two of my best friends and there were times when they would just rather avoid me altogether. Here's Travis.
<v Ira Glass>很难描述这像什么。房间看起来实际上缩小了。当然并不是每一个我的朋友都忘了邀请我，反复地。当然。也许是不舒服的笑声，但他们几乎没注意到我的脸红了，我浑身是汗。我可以想象一个熟人，或一个刚见到我的人，可能会那样看待我。但这是我最好的两个朋友，有几次他们宁愿完全避开我。这是特拉维斯。

00:53:48.770 --> 00:54:00.090
<v Travis>It kind of seems like you intentionally try and be the kind of aggressive guy who makes people uncomfortable, or whatever.
<v Travis>看起来你是有意尝试做那种让人们不舒服的激进家伙，或什么。

00:54:00.090 --> 00:54:01.500
<v Gabriel Delahaye>Andrew chimed in.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>安德鲁附和道。

00:54:01.500 --> 00:54:18.430
<v Andrew>Yeah. I feel like you end up setting the threshold for what people's comfort level with your relationship is. The amount of aggression, or back and forth. And if they don't like it, it's often just, like, that's it.
<v Andrew>是的。我觉得你最终设定了人们对你关系的舒适度的门槛。那种攻击性，或来来回回。如果他们不喜欢，它经常就只是，就这样。

00:54:18.430 --> 00:54:43.900
<v Gabriel Delahaye>I guess I just assumed we all have our flaws. Andrew is the kind of guy who argues everything, and I mean everything. Like the   word grill, which is popular slang for face. Andrew has maintained for years that it is slang for a rib cage, despite a thousand rap songs to the contrary. Or if Andrew's tired, and you're hanging out with him, he just talks about being tired the whole time. And it's like, dude, I get it. You're tired. Go take a nap.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>我猜我只是假设我们都有缺陷。安德鲁是那种争论一切的人，我是说一切。就像“烧烤”这个词，是脸的流行俚语。安德鲁多年来坚持认为它是肋骨的俚语，尽管有成千上万的说唱歌曲反其道而行之。或者如果安德鲁累了，你和他混在一起，他整个时间都在谈论累。就像，伙计，我知道了。你累了。去小睡一下。

00:54:43.900 --> 00:55:13.130
<v Gabriel Delahaye>But that's my point. We're friends, so I accept all that about him. I don't warn people to avoid the word grill around Andrew. I don't tell them in advance that most of Travis' day-to-day conversation is a string of impenetrable one liner inside jokes that he would never bother explaining to someone who didn't understand them already. I accept these things about Andrew and Travis as part of the deal. Just like I expected them to accept that I can be an ass[BLEEP]. Which, actually, I guess they do. I just don't get to decide what that acceptance looks like.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>但这就是我的观点。我们是朋友，所以我接受关于他的一切。我不警告人们在安德鲁面前避免使用烧烤这个词。我不会提前告诉他们，特拉维斯的大部分日常谈话是一串不可穿透的单线笑话，他永远不会费心向已经理解的人解释。我接受关于安德鲁和特拉维斯的这些东西作为交易的一部分。就像我希望他们接受我可以是一个混蛋[哔——]。这实际上，我猜他们确实做了。我只是没有决定那种接受看起来像什么。

00:55:13.130 --> 00:55:21.580
<v Travis>We're friends. We're friends. But some of the time spent with you is not fun.
<v Travis>我们是朋友。我们是朋友。但和你在一起花掉的一些时间是不好玩的。

00:55:21.580 --> 00:55:34.230
<v Andrew>That's really well put. It's not about like or dislike. It's about just how you treat people, I guess.
<v Andrew>那总结得真好。这关于喜不喜欢。这是关于你如何对待人，我猜。

00:55:34.230 --> 00:55:50.500
<v Gabriel Delahaye>When Travis left, it was pretty tense in our apartment. Andrew and I spent half an hour making small talk. Saying things like, "Dinner is a really great meal, you know? Like out of the three?" Just to ensure that our friendship hadn't suffered a mortal blow.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>当特拉维斯离开时，我们的公寓里很紧张。安德鲁和我花半小时进行小谈话。说些话比如，“晚餐真是顿很棒的饭，你知道吗？就像这三顿中的？”只是为了确保我们的友谊没有遭受致命打击。

00:55:50.500 --> 00:56:21.340
<v Gabriel Delahaye>The next morning, I woke up with an emotional hangover. I had that anxiety where it feels like someone is sitting on your chest and I left early for work to avoid seeing Andrew again. I spent the whole day writing emails to people, asking them to remind me that I'm not an ass[BLEEP]. When I told them that I was confronting this reputation head on, the typical reaction was not reassure me, but just to tell me how brave I was for trying. "Oh," one of them wrote. "I don't think I could do what you're doing. That sounds really hard."
<v Gabriel Delahaye>第二天早上，我醒来时带着情绪宿醉。我有那种焦虑，感觉有人坐在我的胸口上，我早早去上班，为了避免再次见到安德鲁。我花了一整天给人们写邮件，请他们提醒我我不是混蛋[哔——]。当我告诉他们我正面面对这个名声时，典型的反应不是安慰我，而只是告诉我为了尝试我有多勇敢。“噢，”其中一个人写道。“我想我做不到你正在做的事。那听起来真难。”

00:56:21.340 --> 00:56:30.890
<v Gabriel Delahaye>Over the next few weeks, I talked to friends and family to see if they felt the same way. And everyone, down to the last man, had something to say on the subject. My brother.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>在接下来的几周里，我和朋友和家人交谈，看他们是否也有同样的感受。每个人，一直到最后一个人，都有关于这个话题要说。我的兄弟。

00:56:30.890 --> 00:56:38.320
<v Gabriel's Brother>You know, it's embarrassing, of course. Sometimes you go a bit over the top.
<v Gabriel's Brother>你知道，这很尴尬，当然。有时你有点过头了。

00:56:38.320 --> 00:56:39.510
<v Gabriel Delahaye>My ex-girlfriend Kate.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>我的前女友凯特（Kate）。

00:56:39.510 --> 00:56:45.770
<v Kate>I think you, honestly, embrace it. I mean, do you like knowing that people say they don't like you?
<v Kate>我认为你，老实说，拥抱它。我是说，你喜欢知道人们说他们不喜欢你吗？

00:56:45.770 --> 00:56:46.600
<v Gabriel Delahaye>My friend Scott.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>我的朋友斯科特（Scott）。

00:56:46.600 --> 00:56:59.120
<v Scott>I didn't like you before I met you. And I had decided that I was not going to talk to you or give you the time of day. And I also tried to persuade other people to dislike you.
<v Scott>在见到你之前我不喜欢你。我决定我不会跟你说话或给你时间。而且我也试图说服别人不喜欢你。

00:56:59.120 --> 00:57:00.110
<v Gabriel Delahaye>Even my mom.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>甚至我的妈妈。

00:57:00.110 --> 00:57:18.020
<v Gabriel's Mom>Well, it used to bother me. Because I thought, wow, all this energy I put into this kid and he makes fun of me. Then I got OK with it. I think that's kind of where I'm at, I'm mostly OK with it.
<v Gabriel's Mom>嗯，它以前困扰我。因为我想，哇，我投入到这孩子身上的所有能量，他却取笑我。然后我对它没关系了。我认为这在某种程度上就是我在的地方，我大部分时间对它没关系。

00:57:18.020 --> 00:57:32.640
<v Gabriel Delahaye>It's easy to dismiss Andrew or Scott. But my mom? How do I dismiss my mom? By the time I talked to my friend Carey, who just decided, like, last Friday that she didn't hate me anymore, she asked me the next logical question.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>忽略安德鲁或斯科特很容易。但我的妈妈？我怎么忽略我的妈妈？当我交谈到我的朋友凯莉（Carey）时，她只是决定，就像，上周五她不再恨我了，她问我下一个合乎逻辑的问题。

00:57:32.640 --> 00:57:36.780
<v Carey>So what are you going to do? Are you going to change?
<v Carey>所以你打算做什么？你会改变吗？

00:57:36.780 --> 00:57:48.500
<v Gabriel Delahaye>Well. Uh.
<v Gabriel Delahaye>嗯。呃。

00:57:48.500 --> 00:58:05.190
<v Ira Glass>It was in that pause right there, when I avoided answering, that I realized what I had done. I believe I'm the first person in history to have staged an intervention on himself.
<v Ira Glass>就是在那停顿中，当我避免回答时，我意识到我做了什么。我相信我是历史上第一个对自己策划干预的人。

00:58:05.190 --> 00:58:31.860
<v Ira Glass>What started out as an attempt to force Andrew into admitting that I wasn't an ass[BLEEP] became a referendum on me as a person, with everyone agreeing that he was kind of right. And while I might not have thrown any furniture, or beaten my fist against the wall, crying out, "How could you all do this to me?" I did what a lot of people in interventions do. I looked my friends and family in the eye, apologized and then politely refused to go to rehab.
<v Ira Glass>开始时试图强迫安德鲁承认我不是混蛋[哔——]，变成了一个关于我作为人的全民公决，每个人都同意他是对的。虽然我可能没有扔掉任何家具，或用拳头撞墙，大喊“你们怎么能都这样对我？”我做了很多在干预中人们做的事。我看着我朋友和家人的眼睛，道歉，然后礼貌地拒绝去康复中心。

00:58:31.860 --> 00:58:59.680
<v Ira Glass>Because, unpleasant as I discovered I can be, I just know in my heart that I'm not changing. And in response to Carey's question, which is the most obvious question to ask, what am I going to do? Why don't I change? Why don't I stop the teasing and the fake punching and the helmet slapping? I don't know. Inertia?
<v Ira Glass>因为，虽然我发现我可能很令人不快，但我心里知道我不会改变。而对于凯莉的问题，这是最明显的问题，我打算做什么？为什么我不改变？为什么我不停止取笑和假打和拍头盔？我不知道。惯性？

00:58:59.680 --> 00:59:15.710
<v Ira Glass>Gabriel Delahaye, is an ass[BLEEP] living in New York.
<v Ira Glass>盖布里埃尔·德拉海（Gabriel Delahaye），是生活在纽约的混蛋[哔——]。

00:59:15.710 --> 00:59:28.860
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Sarah Koenig and myself, with Alex Blumberg, Jane Feltes, Lisa Pollak, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer is Julie Snyder. Production help for our show by Seth Lind and Tommy Andres. Music help from Jessica Hopper. Web help from Jorge Just and Xioa  Jao Yung.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由莎拉·科尼格和我制作，还有亚历克斯·布隆伯格，简·费尔特斯，丽莎·波拉克，阿丽莎·希普，和南希·厄普代克。我们的高级制片人是朱莉·斯奈德。我们节目的制作帮助来自塞斯·林德和汤米·安德雷斯。音乐帮助来自杰西卡·霍珀。网页帮助来自豪尔赫·贾斯特和萧兆阳。

